  "dom_id": "step-q332613",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(3,-1)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(3,-1)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032} & =-x+4y \\\\  & =-3+4(-1) \\\\  & =-3-4 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =2x+2y \\\\  & =2(3)+2(-1) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
